        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f8ebbbbf-7f90-5502-8483-58be7c315d66",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Peter Jackson, Stephen G. Haw, Marco Polo\n- Organizations: Keele University, University of Keele, University College of North Staffordshire\n- Places: Newcastle-under-Lyme, Staffordshire, England, China, East Asia, Iran, India\n- Roles: scholar, historian, Emeritus Professor of Medieval History, editor\n- Products: The Cambridge History of Iran: The Timurid and Safavid Periods\n- Concepts: Crusades, Mongol conquests, Europeans and Mongols contacts, medieval Muslim India, Marco Polo historiography\n\nFacts:\n- Keele University was founded in 1949 as the University College of North Staffordshire.\n- Keele University was granted university status by Royal Charter in 1962.\n- Keele University, officially called the University of Keele, is a public research university located about 3 miles from Newcastle-under-Lyme in Staffordshire, England.\n- Peter Jackson is a scholar and historian who specializes in the Crusades, contacts between Europeans and Mongols, and medieval Muslim India.\n- Peter Jackson is Emeritus Professor of Medieval History at Keele University.\n- Peter Jackson is the editor of The Cambridge History of Iran: The Timurid and Safavid Periods.\n- Stephen G. Haw made a major contribution to studies of Marco Polo's account of East Asia.\n- Peter Jackson described Stephen G. Haw as an authority-relevant voice on the history of the Mongol conquests and said Haw's book on Marco Polo should have settled the controversy over whether Marco Polo visited China.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "67c33953-3e05-5903-8bdf-d8bd627f05a6",
        "text": "Page: Peter Jackson (historian). Sentence: Peter Jackson is a scholar and historian, specializing in the Crusades, particularly the contacts between the Europeans and the Mongols as well as medieval Muslim India.\nPage: Peter Jackson (historian). Sentence: He is Emeritus Professor of Medieval History at Keele University and editor of \"The Cambridge History of Iran: The Timurid and Safavid Periods\".\nPage: Keele University. Sentence: Keele University, officially known as the University of Keele, is a public research university located about 3 miles (5 km) from Newcastle-under-Lyme, Staffordshire, England.\nPage: Keele University. Sentence: Keele was granted university status by Royal Charter in 1962 and was founded in 1949 as the University College of North Staffordshire.\nPage: Stephen G. Haw. Sentence: He has made a major contribution to studies of Marco Polo's account of East Asia: according to Peter Jackson, an authority on the history of the Mongol conquests, his book about Marco Polo \"must surely now have settled the controversy surrounding the historicity of Polo's visit to China.\"",
